# Schema change approval — hard stop

**Never** create, edit, or delete a Mongoose model, Mongo collection, or schema field
without **this conversation’s explicit user approval** of **that exact change**.

If there is any doubt, **stop and ask**. Do not implement first.

A phase doc, plan, ADR, ticket, eval script, or “implement / complete / go ahead”
on a feature is **not** approval. Executing or confirming a plan is still not
approval of a collection, field, or index — stop and get a yes on that exact list
in the same conversation before writing the model. “Looks needed” is **not** approval.
“Use your judgement” does **not** apply to schema. Prior approval of collection `X`
does **not** allow a later field, index, or second collection.

## Locked (ask first — every time)

- New `backend/src/models/*.model.js` files
- New Mongo collections or new `mongoose.model(...)` names
- Adding, renaming, removing, or retyping a field (including nested subdocs)
- Changing `enum`, `default`, `required`, `ref`, `min`, `max`, `maxlength`, `trim`, or `unique`
- Adding, dropping, or changing indexes (including unique / partial filters)
- Changing `createBaseSchema` / base fields (`status`, `orgId`, actors, timestamps)
- Renaming a collection or model
- Updating `docs/data-model.md` or `docs/schema/` to invent fields the schema does not have
- “Temporary”, “stub”, or test-only production collections used as a workaround

## Allowed without asking

- Comments / JSDoc on an **existing** model file when **no** field, type, index, or option changes
- Reading, querying, or importing existing models and existing fields
- Service / route / UI code that uses fields already on the approved schema

## Required stop

1. **Stop.** Do not write the model file, edit the schema, or update `docs/data-model.md`.
2. **Propose** the exact collection name, every field (name, type, required/default), and indexes.
3. **Ask** and **wait** for a clear yes on that proposal in this conversation
   (e.g. “approved”, “yes add those fields”).
4. Implement **only** the approved list. No extra fields “while you are there”.
5. Update `docs/data-model.md` in the **same** change. Add field comments in the **same** change.

Reuse an existing approved collection when possible. Do not invent a second collection
to avoid asking.

## Not approval

- Implementing a plan that mentions a collection
- Matching a phase-doc field list the user has not signed off in this chat
- “Add notifications / usage / retention” without a reviewed field list
- “I’ll add `foo` so the API can store it”
- Editing indexes “just to make the query fast”
- Approval from an older conversation, a different collection, or a different field list

## Anti-pattern

```js
// ❌ BAD — new collection during a feature, no user yes
export const Widget = mongoose.model('Widget', widgetSchema);

// ❌ BAD — extra field on an existing schema without asking
lastNotifiedAt: { type: Date, default: null },
```

```text
✅ GOOD — ask first, then wait
Proposed collection `widgets` with fields: name (string, required), …
Approve, change, or reject?
```
